{{smaller block|The spirits of things animate . . . are ''branched'' into canals as blood is.
{{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist.}}}}
'''2.''' To adorn with needlework; decorate with embroidery; adorn with flowers or other ornament, as in textile fabrics.
{{smaller block|<poem>The train whereof loose far behind her strayed,
''Branched'' with gold and pearl most richly wrought.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser.''}}
Calling my officers about me, in my ''branched'' velvet gown.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., ii. 5.}}
<poem>A dress
All ''branch’d'' and flower’d with gold.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To branch''' (a thing) '''out,''' to make it spread out in divisions like branches. [Rare.]}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ah, my Giacinto . . .
''Branches'' me ''out'' his verb-tree on the slate.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, II. 64.}}}}

'''branch-chuck''' (brȧnch′chuk), ''n.'' In ''mech.'', a chuck formed of four branches turned up at the ends, each furnished with a screw.

